Hi Zhian,
Thanks for helping me get started with poppr! I was wondering about this AMOVA test when you only use the single stratum "Pop" to split your dataset into populations. My understanding in the example you gave:

poppr.amova(Aeut, ~Pop)
##
##  No missing values detected.
##
## $call
## ade4::amova(samples = xtab, distances = xdist, structures = xstruct)
##
## $results
##                  Df    Sum Sq     Mean Sq
## Between samples   1 1051.2345 1051.234516
## Within samples  185  849.9633    4.594396
## Total           186 1901.1979   10.221494

...Is that "samples" should be read as "populations".
However, in this tutorial (http://grunwaldlab.github.io/Population_Genetics_in_R/AMOVA.html)  they state, "This example was performed with a data set of dominant (AFLP) markers, but it can also be performed on codominant markers such as SNPs or SSRs. These provide more information because within sample (individual) variance is also assessed." What I'm wondering is why, when I use a SSR dataset and set within=TRUE, do I still only get the same three levels (between samples, within samples, and total) and not another one for within individuals?

Thanks!
Melanie

